A||N||4||Patrick86GT||coolant sensor info sought||||1||
Z||000000||Patrick86GT||07-23-2000||07:54 PM||||i gota code 15 on my 86gt (2.8); the engine is not overheating in any way, so i checked all leads and connections. after that i went to my local Carquest parts man. Carquest supplied me w/ a dual lead coolant temp sensor & instructed me to join both new leads to the single original wire.  I did that, cleared the ECM of codes, and w/i a few miles of driving, the code returned. I then repeated the process using a single new lead. Again the code returned w/i a few driving miles. But, now it is really compounded. <P>Heretofore, my idle has run 1000 to 1050 RPM, (not perfect, but very acceptable). Now a new problem has entered the brew. My idle has gone random, sometimes running at 2000 to 2500 RPM. Although I have no codes to indicate IAC, I cleaned it: no better. Anybody know what's up w/ this situation?   ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||07-23-2000||09:26 PM||||Check the thread on sensors today. The sensor for the ECM IS a 2wire sensor. You might be messing with the wrong one||||reg||1
Z||000002||Patrick86GT||07-24-2000||10:44 AM||||Thanks for your thought! <P>I may well misunderstand the location of the coolant temp sensor. My Haynes manual is less than detail specific. There are 2 water temp sensors on top right of the engine. One is physically larger and forward of the other. The Online Fiero Service Guide indicates that the CTS is on the SIDE of the manifold (but I can't see one there) while Haynes seems to indicate that it is on TOP.  Letting the Carquest replacement part be my guide for which to replace may be at the root of my problem. <P>Now, I don't know which is the one to replace or what it is that I've incorrectly replaced! Rats! Any input would be appreciated.   ||||reg||1
Z||000003||brianp1fiero||07-24-2000||06:38 PM||||The original two lead temp sensor is a two in one deal.  One side of this sensor reads the water temp.  The other side causes the dash light to come on when the water gets too hot.  Basically one side is a thermistor (resistor that changes value with temp) and the other side is an off & on switch.  Hince the two leads.||||reg||1
Z||000004||brianp1fiero||07-24-2000||06:42 PM||||Oh yeah there is also an off and on sensor that controls the rad. fan.  It has a green wire going to it I believe.  Simply grounding this wire with the key on will cause the fan to run.  ||||reg||1
